## F. Success-conditional vs restricted means (censoring)

The paper's S̄ and P̄ average over successful runs only; with its 100 % success rates this is harmless. Ours has unequal success rates, so conditional means can favour the method that fails more. The restricted mean counts every unsuccessful run at the cap (200 iterations for falsification; 205 Track calls for E6; (M+1)(K+1) = 5,511 Track calls for E5 LCW).

| Comparison | ratio of conditional means, geomean (as reported) | ratio of restricted means, geomean |
|---|---|---|
| E2/E3 S̄ PBO-RTAMT/PROF (>1 favours PROF) | 0.47 | 0.34 (per φ: 0.08, 0.40, 0.62, 0.63, 0.37) |
| E2/E3 S̄ SBO-RTAMT/SMuRF (>1 favours SMuRF) | 1.23 | 1.03 (per φ: 1.40, 0.64, 0.81, 1.63, 0.98) |
| E6 BO/LCW iterations within 205 Track calls (<1 favours BO) | 0.52 | 0.53 (per φ: 1.23, 0.66, 0.24, 0.75, 0.95, 1.18, 0.12, 0.32) |
| E5 φ5 LCW/LCP P̄ (LCW fails 786/1000) | 20.8 | ≥ 38.2 (failures counted at 5,511) |

Reading: a restricted mean is a lower bound for the true mean of a method that fails within the cap.

